Aim: To perform basic Git version control operations and understand collaboration in software development.

Requirements:
- Git installed
- GitHub account
- VS Code or Command Prompt
Procedure:

Step 1: Install Git
1. Visit Git official download page.
2. Download Git for Windows.
3. Install it using the default settings.
4. Open Git Bash or Command Prompt.
Step 2: Check Git version

Execute:
git --version


Expected output: Git version is displayed.

Step 3: Configure Git username and email
git config --global user.name "Your Name"


git config --global user.email "your-email@gmail.com"


Check configuration:
git config --list


Step 4: Create a local repository
mkdir GitProject


cd GitProject


Step 5: Initialize Git
git init


This creates a Git repository in your project folder.

Step 6: Create a README file
echo "# My First Git Project" > README.md


Step 7: Check repository status
git status


Step 8: Add files to the staging area
git add README.md


To add all files:
git add .


Step 9: Commit changes
git commit -m "Initial commit"


Step 10: Create a GitHub repository
1. Open GitHub.
2. Log in.
3. Click the + icon.
4. Select New Repository.
5. Enter repository name: GitProject.
6. Click Create Repository.
Step 11: Connect local repository to GitHub

Copy your GitHub repository URL and execute:
git branch -M main


git remote add origin https://github.com/USERNAME/GitProject.git


Replace USERNAME with your GitHub username.

Step 12: Push code to GitHub
git push -u origin main


Step 13: Verify

Open your GitHub repository and refresh the page. You should see the README file.

Expected Output: A local Git repository is created, committed and successfully uploaded to GitHub.
